---
import MathLayout from '../../../layouts/MathLayout.astro';
import { getPath } from '../../../utils/path';

const lang = 'en';
const currentPath = '/en/university/group-theory';
const title = 'Group Theory';
---

<MathLayout {title} {lang} {currentPath}>
	<article>
		<h1>{title}</h1>
		<p class="intro">Group theory studies the pure form of closed operation systems. It emerges from a shift in perspective: instead of calculating on objects, we now study "the way of calculating" as a new object itself.</p>

		<h2>The Four Axioms</h2>
		<div class="concepts-grid">
			<div class="concept-card card">
				<h3>Closure</h3>
				<p>Operation result never leaves the set</p>
			</div>
			<div class="concept-card card">
				<h3>Associativity</h3>
				<p>$(ab)c = a(bc)$</p>
			</div>
			<div class="concept-card card">
				<h3>Identity</h3>
				<p>$e \cdot a = a$</p>
			</div>
			<div class="concept-card card">
				<h3>Inverse</h3>
				<p>$a^{-1} \cdot a = e$</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<h2>Important Theorems</h2>
		<div class="theorem-card card">
			<h3>Lagrange's Theorem</h3>
			<p>The order of a subgroup divides the order of the group.</p>
		</div>
		<div class="theorem-card card">
			<h3>Sylow Theorems</h3>
			<p>Precise characterization of subgroups of prime power order.</p>
		</div>
		<div class="theorem-card card">
			<h3>Galois Correspondence</h3>
			<p>Field extensions correspond precisely to subgroups of the Galois group.</p>
		</div>

		<h2>Learning Path</h2>
		<div class="learning-path">
			<div class="path-step">
				<span class="step-num">1</span>
				<div class="step-content">
					<h3>Group Basics</h3>
					<p>Definitions, subgroups, cyclic groups, permutation groups</p>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="path-step">
				<span class="step-num">2</span>
				<div class="step-content">
					<h3>Cosets & Quotients</h3>
					<p>Coset decomposition, Lagrange, normal subgroups</p>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="path-step">
				<span class="step-num">3</span>
				<div class="step-content">
					<h3>Group Actions</h3>
					<p>Orbits, stabilizers, Cayley's theorem</p>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="path-step">
				<span class="step-num">4</span>
				<div class="step-content">
					<h3>Finite Groups</h3>
					<p>Sylow theorems, classification of simple groups</p>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="path-step">
				<span class="step-num">5</span>
				<div class="step-content">
					<h3>Galois Theory</h3>
					<p>Field extensions, solvable groups, radical solutions</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="nav-footer">
			<a href={getPath('/en/university')} class="back-link">← Back to University Math</a>
		</div>
	</article>
</MathLayout>

<style>
	.intro {
		font-size: 1.1rem;
		margin-bottom: 2rem;
	}

	.concepts-grid {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(150px, 1fr));
		gap: 1rem;
		margin-bottom: 2rem;
	}

	.concept-card h3 {
		margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0;
		color: #f1f5f9;
	}

	.concept-card p {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: 0.85rem;
	}

	.theorem-card {
		margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
		background: rgba(236, 72, 153, 0.1);
		border-color: rgba(236, 72, 153, 0.3);
	}

	.theorem-card h3 {
		margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0;
		color: #f1f5f9;
	}

	.theorem-card p {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: 0.9rem;
	}

	.learning-path {
		margin-bottom: 2rem;
	}

	.path-step {
		display: flex;
		gap: 1rem;
		padding: 1rem 0;
		border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(99, 102, 241, 0.2);
	}

	.step-num {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: center;
		width: 2rem;
		height: 2rem;
		background: linear-gradient(135deg, #ec4899, #f43f5e);
		border-radius: 50%;
		font-weight: bold;
	}

	.step-content h3 {
		margin: 0 0 0.25rem 0;
		color: #f1f5f9;
	}

	.step-content p {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: 0.9rem;
	}

	.nav-footer {
		margin-top: 3rem;
		padding-top: 2rem;
		border-top: 1px solid rgba(99, 102, 241, 0.2);
	}

	.back-link {
		color: #ec4899;
	}
</style>
